WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.668
-My first guest
is a two-time Oscar winner

00:00:02.668 --> 00:00:03.937
and renowned activist.

00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:06.972
Her virtual climate movement
"Fire Drill Fridays"

00:00:06.972 --> 00:00:08.741
streams live every Friday.

00:00:08.741 --> 00:00:12.111
Here is the one and only
Jane Fonda!

00:00:12.111 --> 00:00:13.413
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:14.881
Jane Fonda!

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:16.482
Oh, my goodness.

00:00:16.482 --> 00:00:18.617
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:00:18.617 --> 00:00:19.919
-Thank you for having me.

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:22.789
-Oh, please. Whenever.
We're so lucky to have you.

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:27.293
I -- You've -- I just said you
dedicated your life to activism.

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:32.232
And I just want to see
what went through your head

00:00:32.232 --> 00:00:36.702
when you heard
the verdict just now?

00:00:36.702 --> 00:00:40.740
-I was so relieved,
so grateful to the jury,

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.742
so grateful to those policemen

00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:44.944
who testified against
one of their own,

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:47.046
testified against Chauvin.

00:00:47.046 --> 00:00:50.483
That's so important. But...

00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:54.286
Right now, the anvil of justice
is hot.

00:00:54.286 --> 00:00:57.690
And we have to seize it
and make sure that we use it

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.392
to bend the arc of history

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:02.695
in the direction
that Martin Luther King said --

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:04.764
in the direction of justice.

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:09.669
But the most important thing
as -- For me, as a white person,

00:01:09.669 --> 00:01:14.907
we white people have to realize
this isn't a zero-sum game.

00:01:14.907 --> 00:01:18.878
You know? They get something,
we lose something -- No.

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:23.616
When Black people can access
healthcare and housing

00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:28.188
and decent education
and living wages and so forth,

00:01:28.188 --> 00:01:32.892
it doesn't take away from us,
from white people, you know?

00:01:32.892 --> 00:01:34.560
It's --

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.331
What we don't realize
is that all of us lose.

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:40.900
We all suffer because of racism.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.403
Never was this more obvious
than during the pandemic,

00:01:44.403 --> 00:01:46.805
you know, when so many --

00:01:46.805 --> 00:01:49.709
Most of the front-line workers
were people of color.

00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:53.412
And they were denied the
personal protective equipment.

00:01:53.412 --> 00:01:56.548
They were not able to stay home
if they got sick

00:01:56.548 --> 00:01:58.417
'cause they couldn't afford to.

00:01:58.417 --> 00:02:04.890
We're all in this life together,
and we have to overcome racism.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.861
-Yeah. I agree.
[ Applause ]

00:02:07.861 --> 00:02:09.896
You've been in so many protests,

00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:14.500
and after seeing the protests
for Black Lives Matter,

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:19.639
what was different about this,
and what stuck out?

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:22.441
-I think for a lot of reasons,

00:02:22.441 --> 00:02:26.879
most of them having to do with
the incident itself, you know.

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:29.916
It's very hard to stay
in one place for nine minutes.

00:02:29.916 --> 00:02:33.753
That guy was committed
to killing George Floyd.

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:35.388
And we all saw it.

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:37.790
And so the outpourings
that happened

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:39.792
all over the country,
all over the world,

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:43.663
and they lasted, were more
diverse than ever before.

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:46.165
I mean there were communities
here in California

00:02:46.165 --> 00:02:47.433
that are all white

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:49.835
that were marching
with Black Lives Matter signs.

00:02:49.835 --> 00:02:52.205
I think that during
the pandemic,

00:02:52.205 --> 00:02:53.872
a lot of us realized --

00:02:53.872 --> 00:02:56.843
Many, many of us realized
for the first time

00:02:56.843 --> 00:03:00.112
how so many people,
especially people of color,

00:03:00.112 --> 00:03:03.049
are barely hanging on.

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:06.752
And our hearts began to open.

00:03:06.752 --> 00:03:09.989
We began to feel empathy.
We need more of that.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:12.491
They were very unusual,
those protests.

00:03:12.491 --> 00:03:14.894
And I think that they've
brought us to where we are now.

00:03:14.894 --> 00:03:16.495
And now we have to keep going.

00:03:16.495 --> 00:03:18.598
-I read an interesting
quote today.

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:19.765
Someone said that this case

00:03:19.765 --> 00:03:23.202
has made activists
out of average citizens.

00:03:23.202 --> 00:03:26.038
Do you agree with that?

00:03:26.038 --> 00:03:28.107
-I hope that's true.

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:30.910
I hope that's true.
People certainly did turn out,

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:35.648
across race, across age, across
ability, people in wheelchairs,

00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:37.717
men and women, across gender.

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:41.687
It was really, really important,

00:03:41.687 --> 00:03:44.357
and I hope
that it will continue.

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:46.225
'Cause we have a long way to go.

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:47.694
-Yeah.

00:03:47.694 --> 00:03:48.994
Do you remember the exact moment

00:03:48.994 --> 00:03:52.031
when you decided you wanted
to become an activist?

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:55.234
-Yeah. It was 1968,

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:56.769
and I was living in Paris,

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:58.804
'cause I was married
to a Frenchman.

00:03:58.804 --> 00:04:02.675
And American G.I.s
who had fought in Vietnam

00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:04.777
started to teach me
about the war.

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:07.747
And they gave me a book to read
by Jonathan Schell

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:09.848
called "The Village
of Ben Suc."

00:04:09.848 --> 00:04:11.183
I read it in one sitting,

00:04:11.183 --> 00:04:14.053
and by the time I was through,
I was a different person.

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:15.454
-Is this true?

00:04:15.454 --> 00:04:20.292
I read that in around 1970,
before you won your first Oscar,

00:04:20.292 --> 00:04:23.562
that you were -- almost decided
to just stop acting

00:04:23.562 --> 00:04:27.366
and just devote your life
completely to activism?

00:04:27.366 --> 00:04:29.502
-Yeah, I --

00:04:29.502 --> 00:04:34.073
You know, I had become a kind of
an organizer, an activist,

00:04:34.073 --> 00:04:36.909
and my celebrity separated me

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:39.212
from the people that I was
working with,

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:42.048
and...

00:04:42.048 --> 00:04:44.717
I just --
I didn't want to do it anymore.

00:04:44.717 --> 00:04:46.218
And this guy,

00:04:46.218 --> 00:04:49.555
he became the mayor of Detroit
at one point, Ken Cockrel.

00:04:49.555 --> 00:04:52.691
He's a Black lawyer in Detroit,
who was my mentor and my friend,

00:04:52.691 --> 00:04:55.294
and he said, "Fonda,
listen to me.

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:57.262
The movement has
plenty of organizers.

00:04:57.262 --> 00:04:58.797
We don't have movie stars.

00:04:58.797 --> 00:05:01.266
You not only have to
stay with your profession,

00:05:01.266 --> 00:05:03.068
but you have to be
more intentional

00:05:03.068 --> 00:05:04.270
about how you do it."

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:07.306
And that's when I started
to produce my own movies.

00:05:07.306 --> 00:05:08.908
-There you go. That's what
I'm talking about right there.

00:05:08.908 --> 00:05:11.310
Absolutely.
-Thank you, Ken Cockrel.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:15.982
-And here we are. We're talking
about "Fire Drill Fridays."

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:18.083
It's streaming every Friday.

00:05:18.083 --> 00:05:20.186
Can you explain to the audience
what this is

00:05:20.186 --> 00:05:23.990
and how you came upon this?

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:26.626
-Well, I was motivated
in the fall of 2019

00:05:26.626 --> 00:05:29.562
by the student climate
protesters globally,

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:31.764
the biggest protests in history.

00:05:31.764 --> 00:05:34.033
And I knew
I wasn't doing enough.

00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:35.902
And I'm famous.
I have a platform.

00:05:35.902 --> 00:05:38.604
I've got a hit series,
"Grace and Frankie."

00:05:38.604 --> 00:05:41.374
Sure helps being an activist
when you have a hit series.

00:05:41.374 --> 00:05:43.175
-Yeah, absolutely.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:43.175 --> 00:05:48.514
And so I decided
to move to Washington, D.C.

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:51.117
It became a project
of Greenpeace.

00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:57.390
And our goal was to reach
the 25 million Americans

00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:59.825
who are alarmed
about the climate crisis

00:05:59.825 --> 00:06:02.995
but haven't done anything about
it 'cause nobody's asked them.

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:05.931
You know? And there are
apparently 13 million of them

00:06:05.931 --> 00:06:08.434
who would engage
in civil disobedience

00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:11.570
and risk getting arrested,
but nobody asked them.

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:14.339
So our target
was the great un-asked.

00:06:14.339 --> 00:06:16.842
And I figured, you know,
I turned 82 at the time,

00:06:16.842 --> 00:06:19.511
and I thought, "Well,
they'll see this old lady

00:06:19.511 --> 00:06:22.414
who, you know, she's getting
arrested and going to jail.

00:06:22.414 --> 00:06:25.951
Maybe they'll think, 'Well,
I'm really concerned about this.

00:06:25.951 --> 00:06:27.219
Maybe I'll do that, too.'"

00:06:27.219 --> 00:06:29.521
And people started coming
from all over the country,

00:06:29.521 --> 00:06:32.191
mostly women,
mostly older women.

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:33.525
-Wow.

00:06:33.525 --> 00:06:35.361
-Washington, Oregon, everywhere.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:36.696
From all over.

00:06:36.696 --> 00:06:41.200
And so when the pandemic hit,
we took it online.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:43.769
And, you know, in 2020,

00:06:43.769 --> 00:06:47.873
we had 9-million-some views
across all platforms.

00:06:47.873 --> 00:06:50.142
People have really stuck
with it. It's wonderful.

00:06:50.142 --> 00:06:52.912
-And how did you come up with
the name "Fire Drill Fridays"?

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:56.983
-Well, about five days
before the very first one

00:06:56.983 --> 00:07:00.252
in October of 2019,
we still had no name.

00:07:00.252 --> 00:07:01.820
And we'd been thinking and
thinking

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:03.255
and trying to come up with one.

00:07:03.255 --> 00:07:07.627
And a documentary was being
made, and the sound man --

00:07:07.627 --> 00:07:09.028
We were packing up to leave,

00:07:09.028 --> 00:07:10.863
because we just couldn't
think of a name,

00:07:10.863 --> 00:07:13.199
and he took his earphones off,

00:07:13.199 --> 00:07:16.501
and he said, "Well, why not
call it 'Fire Drill Fridays'?"

00:07:16.501 --> 00:07:17.636
And that was the name.

00:07:17.636 --> 00:07:19.939
It was the sound man
on the documentary crew.

00:07:19.939 --> 00:07:21.874
-See, and it works,
and it sticks in your head,

00:07:21.874 --> 00:07:24.110
and you remember it,
"Fire Drill Fridays."

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.779
-Greta Thunberg said,
"We have to act like

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:29.581
our house is on fire,"
like it's a real crisis,

00:07:29.581 --> 00:07:31.884
'cause it is.
So we're the fire drill.

00:07:31.884 --> 00:07:33.419
We're going to put out the fire.

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:38.023
-For someone just dipping their
toe in the pool or in the water,

00:07:38.023 --> 00:07:40.826
what would be the first thing
you'd say, "Well, this is how

00:07:40.826 --> 00:07:43.162
you can get involved
to help climate change"?

00:07:43.162 --> 00:07:46.832
-Well, the big focus right now
is, um --

00:07:46.832 --> 00:07:51.203
Us American taxpayers give money
to the fossil fuel industry.

00:07:51.203 --> 00:07:56.675
You know, that's kind of like
dating a guy who beats you up,

00:07:56.675 --> 00:08:00.012
but you keep going out with him
and giving him what he wants.

00:08:00.012 --> 00:08:03.816
You know?
I mean, just think about this.

00:08:03.816 --> 00:08:06.318
Almost 3 million people
have died from COVID

00:08:06.318 --> 00:08:09.154
over the last 15 months.

00:08:09.154 --> 00:08:12.658
Over the same time frame,
three times as many people,

00:08:12.658 --> 00:08:15.294
8.7 million people, have died

00:08:15.294 --> 00:08:18.797
because of fossil-fuel-related
air pollution.

00:08:18.797 --> 00:08:22.735
And we give $20 billion a year
to these guys?

00:08:22.735 --> 00:08:24.569
They are bad guys.

00:08:24.569 --> 00:08:27.473
So one of the first things
we have to do is stop --

00:08:27.473 --> 00:08:28.841
They're called subsidies --

00:08:28.841 --> 00:08:32.978
Stop giving subsidies
to the fossil fuel industry.

00:08:32.978 --> 00:08:36.849
So you can go to
firedrillfriday.com,

00:08:36.849 --> 00:08:39.952
and it will lead you to where
you can find out how to do it,

00:08:39.952 --> 00:08:41.353
how to take action.

00:08:41.353 --> 00:08:44.590
Call your elected officials
and President Biden

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:48.060
and demand that not a penny more
should go to these bad guys

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:51.263
that are killing us
and destroying our future.

00:08:51.263 --> 00:08:54.500
-Jane Fonda, I thank you so much
for not stopping

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:57.569
and for doing this
for over 50 years.

00:08:57.569 --> 00:08:58.604
You're amazing.

